<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Govinda III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 793–<a href="/years/814">814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Govinda III was a king of the Rashtrakuta dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, the son of Dhruva. He led campaigns as far north as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and the Himalayan foothills, defeating the Gurjara-Pratihara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a> and receiving the submission of the Pala ruler Dharmapala. In the south he subdued the Pallavas and other Tamil rulers, bringing Rashtrakuta power to its greatest extent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/govinda-iii.jpg" alt="Govinda III" width="246" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B. Lewis Rice (Benjamin Lewis Rice, 1837-1927) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscription_of_Rashtrakuta_Emperor_Govinda_III_at_Veerabhadra_temple,_Mavali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Govinda III succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha">Dhruva Dharavarsha</a> in 793 CE, but his accession did not go unchallenged. His elder brother Stambha waited until he was assured of the support of a number of feudatories and neighbours and then rose in open revolt. Govinda put down the rebellion of twelve kings headed by Stambha and took his brother prisoner. He treated him leniently and restored him to the Ganga viceroyalty, and Stambha remained loyal for the rest of his life. The Someshvara inscription of 804 names Gamundabbe as the king's chief queen.</p><p>In 800 CE Govinda led a northern campaign from his capital Mayurakhandi, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> district. He obtained the submission of the Gurjara-Pratihara emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a>, the Pala emperor Dharmapala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrayudha">Chakrayudha</a>, the puppet ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanyakubja</a>, and the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> also submitted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a> plates of Govinda III say that his horse drank from a Himalayan stream and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> tasted the waters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> the Paramara dynasty ruled there as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Rashtrakutas, and an inscription of 813 CE records that he conquered Lata, southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and made his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> its ruler.</p><p>According to the Nesarika inscription, some kingdoms came under his influence without war. The King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> submitted without a battle and is said to have sent two statues, one of himself and one of his minister. The Nasari record states that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Keralas</a> of the Tamil country paid him tribute. His control reached from the Vindhyas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a>, while the heart of his empire lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> and Tungabhadra rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Govinda III supported more than one faith. Titles in his inscriptions such as Prithvivallabha, Shrivallabha, Vimaladitya and Atishayadhavala are epithets common in Jain royal usage, and grants to Jain monks and temples continued, in keeping with the dynasty's support of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>. With his support the Jain teachers Virasena and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a> composed the Dhavala, a commentary on the Shatakhandagama, and began the Jayadhavala, which their disciples later completed. Govinda III died in 814 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a>, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> or Lata branch of the dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Govinda III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vatsaraja defeated the Pala king Dharmapala but was routed by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva around 786, and Nagabhata II, after taking Kannauj and defeating Dharmapala near Monghyr, was in turn defeated by <b>Govinda III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…then by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva around 786, yet managed to place Chakrayudha on the throne of Kannauj after both rivals withdrew; around 800 the Rashtrakuta <b>Govinda III</b> marched north and received the submission of both Dharmapala and Chakrayudha, and Nagabhata II defeated Dharmapala near Monghyr and took Kannauj.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Govinda III</b> repeated the feat c. 800, defeating Nagabhata II, while Dharmapala and his client Chakrayudha of Kannauj submitted; Rashtrakuta inscriptions claim that his horses drank from the Himalayan streams.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
